24	/**
25	 * Normalises a raw postal address to the single canonical form under which it is both
26	 * stored and compared.
27	 *
28	 * <p>Keeping this in one place means the stored form and the form used for household
29	 * duplicate detection and the shared household id can never drift apart: two owners
30	 * belong to the same household exactly when their addresses normalise to the same value.
31	 *
32	 * <p>The canonical form trims surrounding whitespace, collapses internal whitespace runs
33	 * to a single space, upper-cases every character, and expands the common street-type
34	 * abbreviations ({@code ST -> STREET}, {@code RD -> ROAD}, {@code AVE -> AVENUE}) that
35	 * appear as whole words. An address consisting only of whitespace normalises to the empty
36	 * string.
